"Chinese medicine matches five basic flavors to the five elements, following the same logic used for organs, emotions, and colors earlier in this unit: sour to wood and the liver, bitter to fire and the heart, sweet to earth and the spleen, pungent to metal and the lung, and salty to water and the kidney. In small, ordinary amounts, each flavor is traditionally said to support its matched organ — a little sourness aids the liver, a little saltiness supports the kidney.": "aef0fbb5b277",
"Chinese medicine matches five basic flavors to the five elements, following the same logic used for organs, emotions, and colors earlier in this unit: sour to wood and the liver, bitter to fire and the heart, sweet to earth and the spleen, pungent to metal and the lung, and salty to water and the kidney. In small, ordinary amounts, each flavor is traditionally said to support its matched organ — a little sourness aids the liver, a little saltiness supports the kidney. The twist is that the same flavor, taken to excess, is said to injure the very organ it normally supports, following the same \"too much of a good thing\" logic covered elsewhere in this course. Excessive salty food is traditionally linked to strain on the kidney and, by extension, on blood vessels — a claim modern nutrition also recognizes in its own vocabulary, linking high sodium intake to blood pressure. Excessive sweet food is linked to strain on the spleen's transforming function, echoing modern concerns about sugar and metabolism. This isn't a coincidence worth dismissing, nor proof that one system copied the other. It's a case of two very different frameworks, working centuries apart with different tools, arriving at a similar practical conclusion: flavor, taken moderately, supports the body; the same flavor, taken in excess, can work against the very function it once supported.": "2e3b0788c47b",
